1
00:00:16,508 --> 00:00:19,194
[Free Skiing] Free skiing, I mean, that's an extreme sport.

2
00:00:19,194 --> 00:00:26,268
Can you tell us a bit what it is because I think not everyone around the globe really knows what that discipline really is.

3
00:00:26,268 --> 00:00:38,720
So free skiing is you're on skis, and the skis look a little bit different from race skis or your typical rental skis because they are twin tips.

4
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:41,062
So the front goes up and the back also goes up.

5
00:00:41,062 --> 00:00:53,476
So that allows you to ski switch backwards and you'll need to ski backwards because it's pretty much like gymnastics on skis, you're doing flips, you're doing spins.

6
00:00:53,476 --> 00:00:58,625
There's a half pipe which is shaped like this and you jump out and back in.

7
00:00:58,625 --> 00:01:03,409
And then there's another discipline called slopestyle, which is jumps and rails.

8
00:01:03,409 --> 00:01:07,689
And then finally, the last discipline is called big air, which is just one big jump.

9
00:01:07,689 --> 00:01:15,178
And in terms of skiing, what makes it different from anything else is that it is very expressive.

10
00:01:15,178 --> 00:01:35,177
It's very creative in terms of gymnastics, right, it's beautiful, it's so difficult, but at the same time, there are set difficulties for different tricks, whereas in skiing there is no score based on only difficulty—it is scored on difficulty, amplitude, execution, variety and style.

11
00:01:35,177 --> 00:01:41,926
So if you do it differently than anybody else, it'll score higher even if you're doing the same trick and everybody will do that trick differently.

12
00:01:41,926 --> 00:01:52,369
And so in that way, it makes it really interesting because it allows for a lot of freedom and creativity and self-expression, and it allows you to kind of personalize your own skiing.

13
00:01:52,369 --> 00:02:03,305
I always like to say that if everybody was wearing black and everybody was doing the same trick and I was watching from a far distance, I would be able to tell who is who, because you can really show your own style and influence into skiing.

14
00:02:03,305 --> 00:02:05,389
So that's what I love so much about it.

15
00:02:05,389 --> 00:02:11,565
[1st Victory] You achieved your first World Cup victory in Italy in 2019 when you were only 15. 2019

16
00:02:11,565 --> 00:02:14,524
What has happened since then?

17
00:02:14,524 --> 00:02:18,724
Wow... A lot has happened since then.

18
00:02:18,724 --> 00:02:40,460
I would say, I think what's really fun is because things have happened so fast I definitely still have the kind of underdog mentality in the sense that I now have enough confidence to enter any competition and have the intention to win, but also not really the age or the time experience to really expect it.

19
00:02:40,460 --> 00:02:45,668
So what that means is I feel like it's created a really healthy mindset for me competitively.

20
00:02:45,668 --> 00:02:50,343
I have won X-Games, I've won world championships.

21
00:02:50,343 --> 00:02:57,928
I've made history as the first person of Chinese descent to ever win X-Games, I'm the first female rookie to ever win three medals at X-Games.

22
00:02:57,928 --> 00:03:02,462
I'm the first female rookie to win ski halfpipe.

23
00:03:02,462 --> 00:03:14,612
[榜样力量] One of my biggest goals in skiing has always been to inspire young girls, especially young Asian-American girls or young girls who don't really see representation in our sport yet.

24
00:03:14,612 --> 00:03:18,240
And so that is really exciting to me because I've always had that as a goal.

25
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:26,312
But being able to see those records being broken and see myself taking those steps towards it is kind of a mind-bending moment for me.

26
00:03:26,312 --> 00:03:36,252
So it's happened really fast, but I'm really grateful for all the people who've been around me, my sponsors, IWC, my family, my mom, my coaches who have supported me the whole time.

27
00:03:36,252 --> 00:03:46,451
[Be Young] Sometimes you feel like as a young person, you feel like, Oh, I have to wait until I'm older before I can really make change or before I can really do things that are important and impactful to the world.

28
00:03:46,451 --> 00:03:56,465
But really, I think it's important to be a young person and to show that age doesn't dictate capability and that experience teaches more than anything else.

29
00:03:56,465 --> 00:04:03,032
And so, yeah, I think that is my biggest goal, and it's something that I wish I had more of growing up.

30
00:04:03,032 --> 00:04:20,437
But also, I'm really grateful because I grew up in a very healthy and supportive environment with my mom and my grandma, who are both incredibly empowered women and who served as the role models that I didn't really see on TV or in this sport or other extreme sports, for that matter.

31
00:04:20,437 --> 00:04:26,262
[Joy of Skiing] So I wanted to be kind of the one to introduce skiing to people to show people the joy of it.

32
00:04:26,262 --> 00:04:32,428
And so that when people think of free skiing, they don't think of an older white man.

33
00:04:32,428 --> 00:04:42,484
They think of a young, worldly biracial girl who is bubbly and likes to do a bunch of different things and has this genuine passion for the sport.

34
00:04:42,484 --> 00:04:45,538
And that was probably my biggest goal.

35
00:04:45,538 --> 00:04:49,870
And now, you know, looking in China, it absolutely has paid off.

36
00:04:49,870 --> 00:04:52,060
Before there was nobody at the ski resorts.

37
00:04:52,060 --> 00:04:54,963
I knew everybody who freeskied in China because there were only a handful of them.

38
00:04:54,963 --> 00:04:59,295
There were probably 20 or 30 people in the whole country who knew about it and who were really doing the sport.

39
00:04:59,295 --> 00:05:01,350
And now, you know, I could never count.

40
00:05:01,350 --> 00:05:07,615
I show up to a resort and there are just lines everywhere and people are with their families and their friends, and everyone's having a great time.

41
00:05:07,615 --> 00:05:09,238
And so it's really exploding.

42
00:05:09,238 --> 00:05:13,283
So I'm really glad to have even made a small impact in that.

43
00:05:13,283 --> 00:05:19,825
[Cultures] You often say that your Asian-American background has shaped your character.

44
00:05:19,825 --> 00:05:24,125
Could you describe that a bit what it really taught you?

45
00:05:24,125 --> 00:05:41,516
I think that growing up between two different cultures helped me a lot with diversity and with understanding and learning in the sense that I was very aware of the similarities and differences of culture from a very young age.

46
00:05:41,516 --> 00:05:55,640
So by the age of three, I was fully bilingual and I was noticing the little differences in the kinds of food, but also in the way that people interacted over dinner or hot water versus cold water, the age-old debate.

47
00:05:55,640 --> 00:06:04,656
And so growing up in that environment, I think, has allowed me to then become more receptive of even more cultures as I grow older.

48
00:06:04,656 --> 00:06:13,796
So now coming to Europe more often or traveling to different cities with kind of different traditions in their own geographical locations.

49
00:06:13,796 --> 00:06:19,037
It has made me more receptive to that and made me appreciate it more, I think.

50
00:06:19,037 --> 00:06:28,659
So it's something that has allowed me to accept differences more easily and something that has allowed me to celebrate cross-cultural friendship more easily.

51
00:06:28,659 --> 00:06:32,367
And so those are both things that I continue to try to promote.

52
00:06:32,367 --> 00:06:35,779
[Empowering女性赋权] What does empowering women mean to you?

53
00:06:35,779 --> 00:06:45,089
I grew up in an all-girls K-8 school, and I loved my experience there so much.

54
00:06:45,089 --> 00:06:47,543
When I started freeskiing, I was the only girl on my team.

55
00:06:47,543 --> 00:06:55,902
And it was such an incredibly harsh culture shock and dichotomy when from Monday through Friday, I would be with all girls.

56
00:06:55,902 --> 00:06:59,213
And on Saturday and Sunday, when I went skiing, I would be the only girl.

57
00:06:59,213 --> 00:07:21,875
And so in that sense, I became hyper-aware of my gender and my expression because I felt like in the beginning, all of a sudden everybody around me was male, and when I looked at the great idols of skiing, all of them were male too, and so kind of feeling, should I be ashamed of my femininity, should I try to be more like the boys?

58
00:07:21,875 --> 00:07:22,650
Should I dress like them?

59
00:07:22,650 --> 00:07:24,024
Should I talk like them?

60
00:07:24,024 --> 00:07:25,766
And would that make me more accepted?

61
00:07:25,766 --> 00:07:27,326
Would that make me better at skiing?

62
00:07:27,326 --> 00:07:49,170
You know, these kind of questions went through my mind at a very young age, and I'm really grateful that I had that super strong and empowering educational environment during the weekdays because I think that it could have been pretty detrimental to my kind of esteem as a female if I hadn't had that foundational support, but I did.

63
00:07:49,170 --> 00:08:03,492
And so instead of kind of crushing it down, I learned to celebrate differences and to kind of communicate and make friends and use skiing as a way to create bonds and to create friendship.

64
00:08:03,492 --> 00:08:15,040
And so that was a big learning opportunity for me, and it was also a way for me to kind of embrace being a woman and to embrace the innate power that we have as individuals.

65
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:18,533
And so it has always been really important to me.

66
00:08:18,533 --> 00:08:21,584
In seventh grade, I gave my first speech on women in sports.

67
00:08:21,584 --> 00:08:28,495
It later got used as a voiceover for an international brand for their International Women's Day commercial.

68
00:08:28,495 --> 00:08:33,243
And since then, you know, in high school, I've led workshops for women in sports.

69
00:08:33,243 --> 00:08:42,097
I've led discussions and workshops about female athletes and their portrayal in the media and how that affects people's perception of female athletes.

70
00:08:42,097 --> 00:08:52,311
I think that these are all really important questions that now more than ever, we should be asking ourselves and questions about inclusion and diversity should always be involved in that conversation as well.

71
00:08:52,311 --> 00:08:58,170
[社交媒体] We know that social media is really influencing and forming the opinions of many people.

72
00:08:58,170 --> 00:09:02,453
Where do you see the benefit of social media?

73
00:09:02,453 --> 00:09:06,745
Social media is a double-edged sword, right?

74
00:09:06,745 --> 00:09:11,526
Because I think that especially in my generation, everybody has social media.

75
00:09:11,526 --> 00:09:14,803
Everybody uses it as their primary source of information.

76
00:09:14,803 --> 00:09:26,599
It's the way that you consciously or subconsciously view what is trendy or what is cool, or how to live your life or kind of what goals to set.

77
00:09:26,599 --> 00:09:37,850
And so that last piece, I think, can be extremely beautiful and extremely inspiring, or it can be extremely detrimental, depending on which side you end up on.

78
00:09:37,850 --> 00:09:43,525
And so in terms of the extremely beautiful side, it shows you what's possible, right?

79
00:09:43,525 --> 00:09:50,543
Like through skiing, a lot of—so many people had never heard of skiing before, and maybe they are scrolling on their discover page or whatever.

80
00:09:50,543 --> 00:09:52,924
And then they see a video of me skiing and be like, wow, this looks great.

81
00:09:52,924 --> 00:09:53,502
This looks fun.

82
00:09:53,502 --> 00:09:54,102
This looks cool.

83
00:09:54,102 --> 00:09:55,590
I want to try this—boom.

84
00:09:55,590 --> 00:09:56,685
Maybe that changed their life.

85
00:09:56,685 --> 00:09:58,033
It would have changed mine, for sure.

86
00:09:58,033 --> 00:10:08,020
And so I think that there's that side or they see someone doing something who they didn't think was possible and suddenly they feel inspired and they think, if she can do it, maybe I can try it too.

87
00:10:08,020 --> 00:10:09,751
Or maybe I can enjoy that too.

88
00:10:09,751 --> 00:10:10,812
She looks like she's having fun.

89
00:10:10,812 --> 00:10:11,711
Why don't I give it a shot?

90
00:10:11,711 --> 00:10:12,369
Right?

91
00:10:12,369 --> 00:10:13,713
That's the best thing.

92
00:10:13,713 --> 00:10:14,662
Life is all about joy.

93
00:10:14,662 --> 00:10:18,550
So they can find all these different ways to find joy through social media.

94
00:10:18,550 --> 00:10:21,247
It expands your perspective, and it shows you what's possible.

95
00:10:21,247 --> 00:10:48,754
On the other hand, there's the toxic, hurtful beauty standards that can impact young women and men everywhere by seeing unrealistic beauty expectations or kind of lifestyles and unrealistic portrayals of joy that make them think maybe if I looked a certain way or had a different amount of money or lived in a different place, or had a different job, or were born into a different setting, then maybe I would be happy.

96
00:10:48,754 --> 00:10:55,130
But I think what social media can really obscure are the beautiful imperfections of reality.

97
00:10:55,130 --> 00:10:57,105
So it's a double-edged sword.

98
00:10:57,105 --> 00:11:00,963
And so in that sense, I always try to make my social media as authentic as possible.

99
00:11:00,963 --> 00:11:11,872
Every few months I'll do like a question-and-answer thing to kind of show the more real sides of myself, because on one hand, I'm not going to post: “Hey guys, I had a bad day. Period.”

100
00:11:11,872 --> 00:11:16,423
And that's it, or like: “Today I fell and it hurt, and I didn't feel too great.”

101
00:11:16,423 --> 00:11:21,046
Like, I'm probably not going to post that, but I do want to kind of show that occasionally.

102
00:11:21,046 --> 00:11:26,136
And so I do want to make an effort to converse with people and really say, you know, I'm not perfect all the time.

103
00:11:26,136 --> 00:11:30,631
And that's what makes everything fun, because if you were perfect all the time, then you would adapt to that and that would be your normal.

104
00:11:30,631 --> 00:11:32,352
And then you wouldn't feel perfect anymore.

105
00:11:32,352 --> 00:11:39,989
And so in that sense, you got to have both sides and social media is a great way to spread information about both.

106
00:11:39,989 --> 00:11:47,214
[网络批评] I couldn't agree more, but there is this other part, you know, when you are criticized, how do you work on this?

107
00:11:47,214 --> 00:11:48,203
How do you act on this?

108
00:11:48,203 --> 00:11:50,670
You know, when you have critical feedback?

109
00:11:50,670 --> 00:11:53,638
When I first started getting hate, I used to search for it.

110
00:11:53,638 --> 00:11:58,445
I think that it's so much easier to hold on to negativity than positivity.

111
00:11:58,445 --> 00:12:00,969
And I really love psychology.

112
00:12:00,969 --> 00:12:08,430
So I've read a lot of stuff about this and how kind of as animals the animal brain goes like: “Oh my god, there's danger.”

113
00:12:08,430 --> 00:12:13,070
It's more beneficial to remember where the danger is because you want to avoid being hunted.

114
00:12:13,070 --> 00:12:18,020
If you can remember where the super sweet-tasting berries were hidden in the forest, that's great.

115
00:12:18,020 --> 00:12:25,912
Bonus points, but like you'd rather remember where the predator's cave is, and so it's a lot easier to remember the negative parts.

116
00:12:25,912 --> 00:12:32,901
And that really is embedded into you because you could have 1,000 really positive messages and maybe ten negative comments.

117
00:12:32,901 --> 00:12:39,130
But you will remember the ten negative comments and you will hold on to them and ingrain them into yourself and repeat them to yourself.

118
00:12:39,130 --> 00:12:42,580
And so it's really easy to get into that cycle when I first started getting hate.

119
00:12:42,580 --> 00:12:44,780
I definitely was looped into that cycle.

120
00:12:44,780 --> 00:12:56,457
But, you know, now I really think that I don't really pay too much mind because at the end of the day, it's my life—like I'm in control of my life.

121
00:12:56,457 --> 00:13:03,193
If you don't like what I'm doing, you don't have my life—like, Oh, you don't like my new trick, then don't do it—like great.

122
00:13:03,193 --> 00:13:05,394
You know, that's good for you.

123
00:13:05,394 --> 00:13:08,818
Everybody's entitled to their opinion, but I'm entitled to not listen as well.

124
00:13:08,818 --> 00:13:17,171
And so it's kind of the sense of I can do my thing and you can like it or you don't have to.

125
00:13:17,171 --> 00:13:19,366
My goal is to spread positivity.

126
00:13:19,366 --> 00:13:22,812
If your goal is to spread negativity, do that.

127
00:13:22,812 --> 00:13:25,448
It's your prerogative, but I'm not going to have any part in it.

128
00:13:25,448 --> 00:13:27,640
So that's kind of my philosophy now.

129
00:13:27,640 --> 00:13:30,552
[Shared value with IWC] And now we talk a bit about the shared values.

130
00:13:30,552 --> 00:13:38,815
We just welcomed you as a member of the IWC family, which values do you think that we share that you share with our brand?

131
00:13:38,815 --> 00:13:44,816
There are several values that I share with IWC, one of them is truly being a world citizen.

132
00:13:44,816 --> 00:13:50,609
Like we mentioned before, it's, you know, IWC is literally International Watch Company.

133
00:13:50,609 --> 00:13:53,107
So it's, you know, it's international.

134
00:13:53,107 --> 00:13:56,498
It's got different people from all over the world.

135
00:13:56,498 --> 00:13:59,823
I think it was 55 countries you have employees from.

136
00:13:59,823 --> 00:14:10,509
And so there are so many different perspectives and cultures and ideas that are going into the design, the creation and the production of all of these gorgeous watches.

137
00:14:10,509 --> 00:14:14,002
And so there is that aspect, which I really love.

138
00:14:14,002 --> 00:14:18,542
[Sustainability] Another is sustainability, which is really important to me, especially as a winter sports athlete, right?

139
00:14:18,542 --> 00:14:22,970
With global warming, our sport is the first one to see the changes and our sport is the first one to go.

140
00:14:22,970 --> 00:14:26,525
Even now, over the years, we can really notice it.

141
00:14:26,525 --> 00:14:28,629
Maybe over the summer or in the fall.

142
00:14:28,629 --> 00:14:34,924
We used to go to a few locations and we'd be able to ski for months, and now we can only ski shorter and shorter every year.

143
00:14:34,924 --> 00:14:43,249
Suddenly, it was over 100 degrees Fahrenheit at a glacier when everybody was supposed to be skiing, and instead people couldn't even leave their houses because there was a heat wave.

144
00:14:43,249 --> 00:14:45,759
And so things like that are really impacting the sport.

145
00:14:45,759 --> 00:14:48,481
So being able to hear about IWC’s initiative.

146
00:14:48,481 --> 00:14:58,255
I was just chatting to someone about it, about there was like a 95% compacted paper watchband that IWC is working on.

147
00:14:58,255 --> 00:15:01,369
And so it looks chic.

148
00:15:01,369 --> 00:15:16,323
You look at it and it's gorgeous and the design is spectacular, but also you know that it is sustainably made and that it is something that is made to be carried through generations and that itself is really environmentally friendly.

149
00:15:16,323 --> 00:15:26,915
It's very beautiful what you say about the way we work and the sustainability topic as well as you just mentioned it, I mean, our watches are built to last for generations.

150
00:15:26,915 --> 00:15:34,950
So everything that goes into the watch on the entire way, from the sketchbook to the wrist needs to be most responsibly done.

151
00:15:34,950 --> 00:15:52,428
And talking about your generation now and talking about your thoughts, what would you and your generation like to change for a better future for us and our planet?

152
00:15:52,428 --> 00:15:57,557
Someone once told me that we don't need one person to do everything perfectly for the planet.

153
00:15:57,557 --> 00:16:02,168
We need everybody to do everything imperfectly, but at least try, right?

154
00:16:02,168 --> 00:16:04,118
We don't need one person to hit 100%.

155
00:16:04,118 --> 00:16:08,636
We need everybody to hit 70%, and that is how impact is made.

156
00:16:08,636 --> 00:16:14,522
So maybe, you know, one day I drink from one plastic bottle and I feel not the best because I should have brought my own bottle.

157
00:16:14,522 --> 00:16:22,264
But you know, it's not that one bottle that is destroying the planet.

158
00:16:22,264 --> 00:16:28,477
It's kind of everybody combined ignoring that kind of aspect.

159
00:16:28,477 --> 00:16:33,390
If everybody cared more, you know, then the difference would be made in that sense.

160
00:16:33,390 --> 00:16:50,148
So it comes differently for different kinds of people, depending on people's lifestyles, you know, choosing different modes of transportation if possible, or eating a little bit differently, if possible, or avoiding fast fashion or buying sustainably made products.

161
00:16:50,148 --> 00:16:55,974
And it looks different for different kinds of people, and that is what's important about it, right?

162
00:16:55,974 --> 00:16:57,700
It's different approaches.

163
00:16:57,700 --> 00:17:03,778
So that's kind of my perspective, especially for young people, because it's our planet to be inherited ultimately.

164
00:17:03,778 --> 00:17:11,953
And as planet change is exponentially growing, then the impact is very urgent.

165
00:17:11,953 --> 00:17:16,577
It's very urgent for us to make change in our generation, before things get out of hand, even more.

166
00:17:16,577 --> 00:17:23,257
I mean, we have a lot of possibilities to choose from, right?

167
00:17:23,257 --> 00:17:30,441
We can choose as you just described it, and these are the little things as well, which matter a lot and to think before we act.

168
00:17:30,441 --> 00:17:34,076
And there is another topic which I would have never guessed.

169
00:17:34,076 --> 00:17:40,518
[Blue Angels] You know that you are a big fan of the Blue Angels of the US Navy Flight Demonstration Squadron.

170
00:17:40,518 --> 00:17:42,035
How come?

171
00:17:42,035 --> 00:17:46,092
Yes, I mean, growing up in San Francisco, the Blue Angels would perform every year.

172
00:17:46,092 --> 00:17:54,562
And I remember when I was probably in second grade they came to our school to talk to us.

173
00:17:54,562 --> 00:17:56,212
And I remember I came home so excited.

174
00:17:56,212 --> 00:18:02,768
I was telling my mom about the different formations they flew in and the number of people they had and the people who I had met.

175
00:18:02,768 --> 00:18:07,297
So the next year, I was pointing them out to my mom and my grandma, and I was telling them all about it.

176
00:18:07,297 --> 00:18:08,830
And it was really exciting for me.

177
00:18:08,830 --> 00:18:16,481
And now, you know, we watch them every year from our balcony because we see them where I live, we’re like right under where they are.

178
00:18:16,481 --> 00:18:19,954
So it's really a spectacular sight to see.

179
00:18:19,954 --> 00:18:23,699
And also, you know, I'm a little bit biased because I am an adrenaline addict.

180
00:18:23,699 --> 00:18:39,320
So given the nature of my sport and also the nature of flying upside down in a plane, like it definitely goes together, maybe at some point, you know, it would be a dream to go in one of the planes myself.

181
00:18:39,320 --> 00:18:41,681
But it is spectacular to watch.

182
00:18:41,681 --> 00:18:47,551
[Meaning of Time] And therefore it's always very interesting to talk about time in your discipline as a free skier.

183
00:18:47,551 --> 00:18:56,055
Time might not be the most important element as you just described it, but what does time mean to you?

184
00:18:56,055 --> 00:19:04,778
I actually have always been really fascinated by time—that was actually one of the first entries in my journal, and I write about it quite frequently.

185
00:19:04,778 --> 00:19:16,523
And I think that ever since I was little, I've always been very aware of the passage of time, which I know is an odd topic for an eight- or nine-year-old to be talking about, but I would often bring it up with my mother.

186
00:19:16,523 --> 00:19:30,716
And she was like: “Are you OK?” or something like: “Why is this what you're thinking about? Why are you not thinking about like toys or something? Like, why are you talking to me about like inevitable passage of time—you are a child?”

187
00:19:30,716 --> 00:19:32,617
But I always thought it was so fascinating, right?

188
00:19:32,617 --> 00:19:34,613
Because it's the one constant.

189
00:19:34,613 --> 00:19:35,909
It should be the one constant.

190
00:19:35,909 --> 00:19:41,851
And yet it doesn't feel like that because, you know, when you're little, one year feels like so much.

191
00:19:41,851 --> 00:19:49,746
And right now, especially as I'm growing older and I'm going through my teenage years, I'm growing into myself and becoming more self-aware during my adolescence.

192
00:19:49,746 --> 00:19:58,998
I think that I'm hyper-aware of all the changes that are occurring in my own life and the relationship of time to those changes.

193
00:19:58,998 --> 00:20:02,629
So I think that it's really such a fascinating thing.

194
00:20:02,629 --> 00:20:11,088
And you know, the whole “time flies when you're having fun” and growing up as a runner and also a skier.

195
00:20:11,088 --> 00:20:18,390
I used to think about this all the time because if I'm running for an hour, it feels like a pretty long time if I haven't been training.

196
00:20:18,390 --> 00:20:21,798
But when I start training again and I get in better shape.

197
00:20:21,798 --> 00:20:23,947
Suddenly, I feel like, whoa, like it's a breeze—an hour.

198
00:20:23,947 --> 00:20:24,930
It feels so nice.

199
00:20:24,930 --> 00:20:27,139
And then when I'm skiing, it feels like a blink of an eye.

200
00:20:27,139 --> 00:20:28,197
Like, I don't even know.

201
00:20:28,197 --> 00:20:29,679
I do like a few tricks.

202
00:20:29,679 --> 00:20:31,739
I'm talking to my friends and suddenly an hour is gone.

203
00:20:31,739 --> 00:20:34,239
And so really, being able to keep an eye on it, I think, is something that I am very fascinated in, and that's why it's really exciting to be partnering with IWC.

